  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A few lingering showers today.\n\nCurrent satellite imagery displays a mature, anomalously strong low \npressure system centered over the southern Jersey shore. This low \nwill continue to fill as it drifts eastward today. A few spotty \nlight showers or some drizzle at times will remain across the \nregion this morning, perhaps lasting into this afternoon across \nthe North Country. A northerly flow and lingering low level  \nmoisture will maintain a blanket of clouds through tonight, with\nperhaps some sunshine emerging Tuesday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warming trend mid-week ahead of the next rain shower \nbearing cold front.\n\nOur wind flow will become southerly by Wednesday of this week, \nallowing a warm autumn airmass to push into our region. In \nconjunction with a ridge aloft, and 850 hPa temperatures rising into \nthe upper teens, surface high temperatures will become some 10 \ndegrees or more above normal, with Thursday the likely warmest day \nthis week. Models have slowed a bit with the approach of the next \ncold front with the suite of ensembles (ENS, GEFS, and GEPS) now \nfocusing highest chances for measurable rainfall now Thursday night. \nThis rainfall is also expected to be light, with chances for over a \ntenth of an inch of rain in a 6-hour window generally less than 25 \npercent.",
